Stanton McCandlish writes:
>
>Last neuroscience stuff I read suggested that individual neurons themselves
>don't store info at all.  Only large groups of neurons do that.  A bit like
>slime molds.  Maybe some reading is in order?

Well, there's distributed coding, which means that a specific "symbol" or
"equivalence class" is not represented by a single neuron (a 'grandmother'
cell) but by a distributed pattern of activities.  Many people have a problem
with this because they say you cannot learn such a representation - because no
neuron knows whether to correlate two events or not.  I disagree, each neuron
only needs to correlate local information - as long as a given pattern always
elicits an appropriate response...  There's also the notion that each
neuron does not store an absolute value but a ratio relative to its
neighbors.  This can be accomplished by lateral inhibition and allows a much
greater range to be represented.

But anyway, the neurons still do have to store something, whether it is a
discrete bit or meaningful symbol or just part of a distributed pattern...
So it depends on your definition of `info'.

Paul


