Peter McCluskey writes:

>  There is certainly a lot of complexity in neural behavior that is not
> captured by ANNs, but as far as I can tell very little of it affects
> the storage capacities. Can you provide a more specific reference to
> something that suggests that neurons have more storage capacity than
> an ANN with 7-bit accuracy in its inputs and outputs, or are you just
> saying that there's a lot we don't know?

My particular objection involves the meaning of "storage capacity".
You gave one possible meaning for that -- number of patterns that
can be distinguished.  However, for such a definition to be applied
to processing units more complex than perceptrons, you have to
define what it means to distinguish patterns, and what the information
content of such an operation would be.  Then, once an adequate
capacity definition is developed, we can look at biological neurons
to see how they do.

But even more than this must be done.  My skepticism is based on
the fact that biological neurons exhibit extremely complex behavior.
If your (radical and in need of support) claim -- that seven bits
of information per synapse is an upper limit on the storage capacity
of biological neurons -- is to be tested, you have to show that
your capacity definition is the CORRECT one; ignoring aspects
of neural function that do not fit into your abstract model is
hazardous at best and it is wrong to put the burden of proof on
those who do not accept such simplification without proof.

derek


