Date: Mon, 10 May 93 13:15:33 -0400
From: pavel@PARK.BU.EDU (Paul Cisek)
Subject: LIFE-X: the 120 yr limit, cryogenics, blah

Peter McCluskey writes:
>
> There is certainly a lot of complexity in neural behavior that is not
>captured by ANNs, but as far as I can tell very little of it affects
>the storage capacities. Can you provide a more specific reference to
>something that suggests that neurons have more storage capacity than
>an ANN with 7-bit accuracy in its inputs and outputs, or are you just
>saying that there's a lot we don't know?
>

I think I can answer that.  A real biological neuron fires when the
post-synaptic potentials that travel down from the synapses to the 'axon
hillock' overlap sufficiently to exceed a threshold above which a
self-regenerating reaction occurs.  This is affected by both the amplitude of
the individual spikes and the time delay between them.  The amplitude of each
spike at the synapse is determined by a number of variables like the
concentration of Calcium and various complex proteins, which may therefore be
used to 'store' information in a sense.  As the signal propagates down the
dendrite it decays exponentially, so the distance that must be traveled (a
structural variable) also determines the neuron's likelihood of firing.  The
width of the dendrite segments also has an effect.  The time delay between
spikes is determined also by various biochemical concentrations at the
synapse, as well as by the properties of the cell membrane.  As far as the
chemical concentrations are concerned, there are various amplifying and
inhibiting enzymes present; the construction of some of these from DNA may also
be guided by neurotransmitters released from other neurons.  In short, there
is a perplexing number of places that a neuron can store information over a
long term (please excuse my tirade).

Then there's the notion of automatic gain control - that neurons
with a limited range of firing frequencies can represent a huge dynamic range
of meaningful values when placed within a field of laterally inhibiting
neurons.  For example, your retina can discriminate 9 orders of
magnitude, down to individual photons!  Substantially more than your 7-bit
accuracy.  This range is determined by the connectivity, which is in turn
determined by the variables I mention above...

ANNs assume the existence of a 'point neuron', an abstraction analogous to
point masses in physics.  If this abstraction is correct then our speculations
are reasonable.  However, not knowing, we are foolish if we don't exercise any
caution and confidently announce that our network experiences human
phenomena...

Paul


