Date: Mon, 10 May 93 13:01:20 CDT
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: Nanotech Beanstalks?

An object hanging at the bottom of an orbital suspension bridge
is moving more slowly than orbital velocity at that height.
The gravitational attraction of the Earth is not affected
by the horizontal velocity, but the centrifugal acceleration
(v^2/r) is.  In circular orbit, these accelerations are equal
and opposite, so the net is zero.  On the suspension bridge,
you have an excess of gravity realtive to centrifugal 
acceleration, so you see a net downward force.  You appear to
be hanging from the bottom of the cable.

The mass of a semi-beanstalk depends on the allowable loads
you design to and the amount of redundancy you build into
the structure.  In theory, a material with a strength of
1 million psi and a density of 0.66 lb/in^3 (i.e. the best
existing carbon fiber), would have a mass of cable:mass of
payload of 4000:1.  

An increase of 20% in strength would allow the mass ratio
to fall to 1000:1, so you can see the mass is very sensitive
to strength.  These types of mass ratios (a few thousand to
one) are considered feasible because they are similar to
the weight of a suspension bridge relative to the weight
of a vehicle crossing the bridge.  It requires many trips
on both types of bridges to justify their construction -
an orbital suspension bridge for one trip is a losing
proposition

Dani Eder

