Date: Mon, 10 May 93 11:29:18 PDT
From: danielg@autodesk.com
Subject: CRYONICS: Re: Reviving a neuro suspendee

Hal Finney described the problems of bringing back a nerosuspendee.
I enjoyed his posting very much.  It was very well thought out and
presented.  I suspect however that there may be a subtle flaw in his
reasoning which could invalidate his conclusion that the process is
doomed to fail.

Let's assume that we only have the head and are trying to match up the
nerve ends with those of an unfamiliar body (or simulated nerves with
a simulated body which is the same problem).  We do the back
tracing into the brain to see roughly what each one is for.  Hal seemed
to imply that the match needed to be perfect to be of any use, and that
attempts to get it right would be torture.

I agree that to attempt to get it perfect would be torture, but I don't
believe that we need to do that.  I think that "close is good enough".
The mind will learn and adapt to it's new inputs.

In support of this belief I'd site the experiment in which a subject
would wear a prism over his eyes which would flip his vision up-side-down.
After a day or so, he adjusts quite well to the new view.  The wierd
thing is that after removing the device, he again sees everything
up-side-down for a while and needs to get used to his original eyes again.

The fact that the human mind can make such a huge adjustment so easily
leads me to suspect that it could do the same with slightly misplaced
nerve connections.  Sure, the relearning process might be frustrating
and slow (probably much like that experianced by stroke patients) but
I would gladly go through it in order to live.

- Daniel Green
